<DetailLayout
  title="From a long ride to a broader question."
  eyebrow="Project story"
  intro="Ride for Parkinson's began as an awareness effort and became a catalyst for real-world technology experimentation: live-tracking prototypes, mobile-development questions, and how to make sense of observations over time."
>
  <section>
    <h2>The 711-mile ride</h2>
    <p>
      The ride project connected cycling, Parkinson's awareness, and a
      long-standing interest in practical technology. The ride's wider mission
      included an AI-assisted knowledge resource for patients and families.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Ride for Parkinson's source includes a route map, a browser-based
      phone tracker, and Firebase-backed public tracking code. Its records
      discuss native mobile development and a future educational AI resource.
      Source artifacts establish prior prototyping, not a verified operating
      tracker, working AI system, completed ride, or formal research study.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>A starting point, not proof</h2>
    <p>
      It prompted a broader question: how could published evidence, experience
      over time, and emerging technology help people understand health questions
      without confusing their different roles?
    </p>
    <p>
      Meridian Station grew from that connection, not from a claim that ride
      observations proved a treatment effect. One person's experience is a
      starting point for questions, not a substitute for other perspectives or
      scientific evidence.
    </p>
